{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Mild positive bias overall: model levels tend to run ~0.2–1.5 EUR above realized prices on most non-spike days; apply a small downward level shift, especially in the mid-horizon.",
    "Over-optimistic persistence after rises: when the recent 5–10d trend is up, the model extrapolates too strongly and stays too high; dampen trend-following (shrink forecast changes toward 0) for horizons ~t+5 to t+25.",
    "Systematic overshoot on peaks: local highs are forecast too high and too long (e.g., repeated 69–71 prints vs realized rolling over sooner); cap/dampen forecasts when they exceed recent 10–15d highs by >~1 EUR.",
    "Missed late-horizon upside breakouts: at longer horizons (~t+25 to t+30) the model can underpredict sharp upside moves (e.g., true jumps to ~71–72 while forecast stays ~66–70); add a volatility/regime-switch uplift when recent volatility is rising and price is near the upper end of the 30d range.",
    "Turning-point lag: model is slow to recognize reversals (both downswings after rallies and renewed rallies after consolidations); increase mean-reversion weight when forecast path remains monotone while recent realized prices show flattening/inflection."
  ]
}
